---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI automated builds" activeNav="/guides/cli/builds/automated-builds">
  <h1>Automated builds</h1>
  <p>
    An automated build is one the platform dispatched to a CI provider. Triggering a pipeline
    creates the build record, the provider runs the workflow, and the platform keeps the record
    in step with what the provider reports. Your job is to read these builds, not to write to
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Triggering one</h2>
  <p>
    Automated builds start from a pipeline, not from <code>builds create</code>. Check which
    inputs a pipeline needs, then run it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines inputs --integration-id int-002 pipe-001

appmctl pipelines run --integration-id int-002 pipe-001 \\
  --ref main --inputs '{"profile":"production"}'

# build_id:          bld-004
# status:            pending
# reporting_mode:    automatic
# dispatch_status:   dispatched
# external_run_url:  https://github.com/acme/customer-app/actions/runs/1234567
# request_uuid:      33333333-4444-4555-8666-777777777777
# created_at:        2026-08-03`} />
  <p>
    The response is the build record the dispatch created, so the run already tells you what to
    watch. See <a href="/guides/cli/integrations/pipelines">Pipelines</a> for the full command.
  </p>

  <h2>What you cannot do</h2>
  <p>
    Manual writes are refused with <code>409</code>. The record mirrors the provider, and two
    writers disagreeing about one build would make its history meaningless:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>builds patch --status ...</code></td><td><code>409</code> — the provider owns the status</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>logs post</code></td><td><code>409</code> — the log comes from the provider</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stream</code></td><td><code>409</code> — manual reporting mode only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>builds patch --tag ...</code></td><td>Allowed — tags are platform-side metadata</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>artifacts upload</code></td><td>Allowed — artifacts are attached to the record, not reported by the provider</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>builds cancel</code></td><td>Allowed — requests cancellation through the provider</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    If you find yourself wanting to write status to one of these builds, the build you want is
    a <a href="/guides/cli/builds/manual-builds">manual build</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Watching a dispatched build</h2>
  <p>
    <code>wait</code> is the whole point of these builds from a scripting angle: dispatch, then
    block until the provider is done, and let the exit code decide what happens next.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json pipelines run --integration-id int-002 pipe-001 --ref main |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)

# 0 completed, 1 failed, 130 cancelled, 124 timed out
appmctl builds wait "$BUILD_ID" --timeout 3600`} />
  <p>
    Pass <code>--request-uuid</code> derived from the CI job id to make the dispatch idempotent,
    so a retried job attaches to the same build instead of starting a second one.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancellation</h2>
  <p>
    <code>cancel</code> asks the provider to stop. The record moves to
    <code>cancel_requested</code> and only reaches <code>cancelled</code> once the provider
    acknowledges, so a status read straight after cancelling will usually still show the
    intermediate state:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds cancel "$BUILD_ID"
appmctl builds get "$BUILD_ID"   # status: cancel_requested`} />

  <h2>Reading the outcome</h2>
  <p>
    <code>dispatch_status</code> and <code>external_run_url</code> are what distinguish these
    builds from manual ones: the first says whether the platform managed to hand the job over,
    the second links to the provider's own run page:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json builds get "$BUILD_ID" |
  jq '.data | {status, reporting_mode, dispatch_status, external_run_url}'

# {
#   "status": "failed",
#   "reporting_mode": "automatic",
#   "dispatch_status": "dispatched",
#   "external_run_url": "https://github.com/acme/customer-app/actions/runs/1234567"
# }`} />
  <p>
    A build that failed to dispatch never ran at all — that is a platform or credentials
    problem, not a build failure, and the provider has no run page for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs and artifacts</h2>
  <p>
    Logs arrive from the provider, so read them the same way as any other build. Artifacts are
    usually uploaded by the workflow itself using a CI-scoped key:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json logs get --build-id "$BUILD_ID" | jq -r '.data.content'
appmctl artifacts list --build-id "$BUILD_ID"`} />

  <h2>Tagging from outside</h2>
  <p>
    Tags are the one write that always works, which makes them the way to annotate provider
    builds from your own tooling — a release marker, a ticket number, a channel:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds patch "$BUILD_ID" --tag release:#22c55e --tag JIRA-4821`} />
  <p>Remember that <code>--tag</code> replaces the whole set rather than adding to it.</p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>pipelines:run</code> to dispatch, <code>builds:read</code> to watch,
    <code>logs:read</code> and <code>artifacts:read</code> to inspect the results.
    <code>builds:write</code> is needed only for tags and cancellation.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
